%I A400745 #31 Nov 24 2026 20:12:42
%S A400745 3,7,40,287,2503,23442,222896,2005219,17334805,144296332,1213771546,
%T A400745 10628925091,97995851190,922053835552,8546371121024
%N A400745 Number of primes p <= 10^n whose trajectory under the map p -> prime(A007953(p)) ends in the cycle (3, 5, 11).
%C A400745 Write s(p) = A007953(p) for the sum of the digits of p; the map sends a prime p to prime(s(p)).
%C A400745 Every prime ends in one of exactly two cycles under this map: (3, 5, 11) or (7, 17, 19, 29, 31). For p >= 1000 the map strictly decreases p, because prime(k) < k*(log(k) + log(log(k))) for k >= 6; so every trajectory falls below 1000, where all trajectories were checked.
%C A400745 The complementary count, of primes p <= 10^n ending in (7, 17, 19, 29, 31), is A006880(n) - a(n).
%C A400745 The cycle is 3 -> prime(3) = 5 -> prime(5) = 11 -> prime(2) = 3, so every trajectory ending in it passes through 5 on every turn.
%C A400745 Since s(p) == p (mod 9), s(p) is divisible by 3 exactly when p is. As 3 is the only prime divisible by 3, no prime other than 3 has a digit sum divisible by 3, and no prime has digit sum 1. Hence the map never reaches the primes at positions 1, 6, 9, 12, ..., that is, 2 and prime(3k) for k >= 2. So at least floor(m/3) of the first m primes are never reached, exactly floor(m/3) for 3 <= m <= 2200 (explicit primes with each other digit sum up to 2200 were found), and the proportion tends to 1/3 (Drmota, Mauduit and Rivat).
%C A400745 Accordingly the cycle elements occupy only positions not divisible by 3, except position 3: 3, 5, 11 are prime(2), prime(3), prime(5), and 7, 17, 19, 29, 31 are prime(4), prime(7), prime(8), prime(10), prime(11). Position 3 (the prime 5) is reached only from 3, which already lies on the cycle; so 5 is a permanent member of the cycle but is never the point of entry for any prime other than itself. Trajectories from outside enter at 3 or at 11.
%C A400745 Along every trajectory the position of the next prime is congruent to the current prime mod 9; for example 31 -> 7 = prime(4), and 31 == 4 (mod 9).
%e A400745 a(1) = 3: the primes up to 10 are 2, 3, 5, 7. 2 -> prime(2) = 3, while 3 and 5 lie on the cycle (3, 5, 11); 7 lies on the other cycle.
%e A400745 For p = 359: 359 -> prime(17) = 59 -> prime(14) = 43 -> prime(7) = 17, which lies on (7, 17, 19, 29, 31).
%o A400745 (Python)
%o A400745 from sympy import prime, primerange
%o A400745 def a(n):
%o A400745     c = 0
%o A400745     for p in primerange(10**n + 1):
%o A400745         q = p
%o A400745         while q not in (3, 5, 11, 7, 17, 19, 29, 31):
%o A400745             q = prime(sum(map(int, str(q))))
%o A400745         c += q in (3, 5, 11)
%o A400745     return c
%Y A400745 Cf. A000040, A006880, A007953.
%K A400745 nonn,hard,more,new
%O A400745 1,1
